  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...As we get into tomorrow and Wed, less cloud \ncover and warmer temps as highs get into the upper 80s to mid \n90s. Combined with dewpoints in the mid to upper 70s each day, \nheat indices could range from 105-109F each afternoon across a \nlarger footprint of ENC. As a result could see a broader heat \nheadline tomorrow for ENC once the ongoing heat advisory ends. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...An unsettled pattern will persist over the next few \ndays with several factors forecast to increase shower and \nthunderstorm chances this week. \n\nFrontal boundary remains stalled across the Albemarle Sound and\nthe northern Outer Banks late this evening. Earlier convection\nhas dissipated, but showers and storms now approaching the\ncoastal plain from the west. Overall svr threat remains low the\nnext few hours, with better chances for moderate to heavy rain. \nTonight, the outflow boundary dissipates and the frontal \nboundary lifts back N, \"warm- sectoring\" the FA once again for \nTue and Wed. \n\nTue features slight dip in available moisture with drier 850-700mb \nlayer getting pushed up the SECONUS coast ahead of the next upper \ntrough which will be pushing SE across the Ohio River Valley, \nleading to more typical summer-time PoPs of 20-40% through Tue \nnight. \n\nA strong front is forecast to approach and cross the area late Wed \ninto Thu, posing the next threat for severe weather. Upper level \npattern for this frontal passage appears more supportive of stronger \nstorms with a sharper trough aloft digging further S than this \nweekend's trough. Moisture increases ahead of this front with low to \nmidlevel flow opening up moisture advection channel from the E side \nof what is now Tropical Depression 2. The National Hurricane Center \ncontinues issuing advisories for TD2, but poses no tropical threat \nto ENC at this time, as it is forecast to meander slowly W across \nthe Nern Gulf of America.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Wed/Thu stronger front is forecast to make it \nthrough the entirety of the land area of ENC, but still stall \noffshore. This will lead to mostly N-NEerly flow late week into the \nweekend, cutting down on the heat (low to mid 80s/upper 60s splits \ninto the weekend). Severe threat appears minimal behind the midweek \nfront, but the multi day period of showers in the forecast could \nlead to hydro issues.",
